ram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-70-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2064012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3f37ec4813f0566ec982f219a736b11d88f7fb40a1f50ac38ea9c5221cf92eea","compact":"5a5ea29caf3b058cb5c59d5c7d58abb34ac288c5bfcffbd61ad5a34860bcb737","nasal":"22a1a34b653dafc9cce13f7317f0c8acc9703e4900d3d7a6e5565e40b6ef6224"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-64-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":64,"verse":13,"english":"Asked by the envoys, I shall, O monarch, go to my father. I shall come again whenever you will remember me.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-70-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2064013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9ea81aed6f93135fb34a41ed41a44a711213e334965993cd7bf66c03e6aee9ca","compact":"c070e2ce68e09007a22f5696e6b38cbbbbda3f63fa0c6b8df767b92ebb4eff69","nasal":"c070e2ce68e09007a22f5696e6b38cbbbbda3f63fa0c6b8df767b92ebb4eff69"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-64-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":64,"verse":14,"english":"Thus accosted by Bharata, his maternal grandfather, the king, smelling Bharata's head, spoke these auspicious words to that descendant of Raghu.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-70-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2064014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2a6ae1afdfe4f6ba7f883e681033d79b29e93e678dc2200f743ffcb53249b8d7","compact":"04137b4746532734ed44032668a690691059feb7edef651ac154d24249412e08","nasal":"04137b4746532734ed44032668a690691059feb7edef651ac154d24249412e08"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-64-15","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":64,"verse":15,"english":"Go, my child; I permit you. Kaikeyi is mother of a worthy son through you. Do you O subduer of foes, communicate our welfare to both your father and your mother.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-70-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2064015","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b9a96c9b0dfd3ed91c4330de8e60f7498740b927d0f0450cbf45d15259d02200","compact":"dc893dd199df31745666b9c3aa8363a2e1ed7aad5ee4765a6a7ec3318228abd7","nasal":"dc893dd199df31745666b9c3aa8363a2e1ed7aad5ee4765a6a7ec3318228abd7"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-64-16","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":64,"verse":16,"english":"Do you likewise communicate the same to the priest and the other principal Brāhmaṇas; as also, my child, to those mighty bowmen, the brothers Rāma and Lakşmaņa.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.867,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-70-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2064016","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cae75731712803a91ee42623839edee9e3407888f03c552f4c51b6323de33ad3","compact":"aab1bc6aaccb140ba6db90879595bf8b6e6c705b48d97d3269c2bf9f6964fe85","nasal":"aab1bc6aaccb140ba6db90879595bf8b6e6c705b48d97d3269c2bf9f6964fe85"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-64-17","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":64,"verse":17,"english":"Then honouring Bharata, king Kekaya conferred on Bharata wealth consisting of choice elephants maintained (at the palace) and woollen sheets and deerskins.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.587,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-70-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2064017","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8cdb512368b8aa091807916fec57cdd78b7d806df4c0408c5c7c0ef667c3d1c4","compact":"ccb3db42e59500ef868e53239bd9bc70dff8c9c01ef8a14f3134736271631100","nasal":"1761107c20fc509845a369571b84c7b80043a1966ea89958bba0e8983727ba1f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-64-18","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":64,"verse":18,"english":"Honouring the son of Kaikeyi, Kekaya gave him two thousand nishkas and six hundred : horses.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.705,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-70-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2064018","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2b42b05c51be8dd94298d2b5f537f247957b90477bc9952b641b3e9c20322695","compact":"adc0eb91356c2db9238cd7433f22aa7602ae1a5180132ce5b4b4b20be194d19a","nasal":"adc0eb91356c2db9238cd7433f22aa7602ae1a5180132ce5b4b4b20be194d19a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-64-19","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":64,"verse":19,"english":"And for following Bharata, Aśvapati without delay assigned a number of goodly, trustworthy, and qualified courtiers.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.971,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-70-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2064019","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1cda17ec43d6484b397d053d62e81bbf3b7e934a6448eced4f0420af6b8878db","compact":"84f217bbfc9cce723e71175df1f4096694510a4a1568bbc5ccb04b90a4bbfd3d","nasal":"0e6c7418e0587bf94e5f5cc0e5adfd7d6cc8e89d11a91784079a6510aefa59d9"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-64-20","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":64,"verse":20,"english":"Bharata's maternal uncle conferred on Bharata wealth in the shape of graceful elephants sprung in the īrāvat mountain and the country called Indrasira; as well as fleet and well-broken horses.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.878,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-70-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2064020","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"48cfaa5a385a201276745bcf1266a72ad1ce23a50fc4b0bd2d4d7281b1953e8e","compact":"b86f2395221fa9ce0752540f